# ripgrep defaults. https://github.com/BurntSushi/ripgrep/blob/master/GUIDE.md
#
# ripgrep only reads this file when RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH points at it — there is
# no implicit lookup by name or location. The shell config is what sets it:
#
#   RIPGREP_CONFIG_PATH=$HOME/.ripgreprc
#
# If that export is missing, every line below is silently inert and rg falls
# back to its own defaults. Check with `rg --debug --files 2>&1 | head -1`,
# which names the config file it loaded.
#
# One flag per line. Shell quoting is NOT applied here, so a value with spaces
# goes on its own line under the flag rather than in `--flag="a b"`.

# Print `path:line:match` on every result line instead of grouping matches under
# a path heading. Terminals and editors that linkify `path:line` — VS Code's
# integrated terminal most of all, since `code -w` is $EDITOR here — can only
# make a hit clickable when the path is on the same line as the match.
--no-heading

# Minified files and lockfiles otherwise blow a single match into a wall of
# text; show a preview of the long line instead of dumping it.
--max-columns=150
--max-columns-preview

# Drop trailing/leading whitespace from matched lines, so deeply indented code
# lines up instead of pushing the match off the right edge.
--trim

# Lowercase query means case-insensitive; type a capital and you meant it.
--smart-case
